## Values in, tensors out

Evaluation takes a `ValueMap`: a map from node id to an `Input` tensor for every free quantity
(parameters and unobserved stochastic quantities). Every tensor is **batched**: a point is
`[batch, storage]`, and results come back as `[batch, 1]`. One call evaluates `batch` independent
settings of the model at once.

> [!NOTE] Storage vs. dimension
> A rotation is *stored* as 9 floats but has *dimension* 3. Points use storage size; tangent vectors
> and derivatives use dimension. See [Tensors and backends](../../concepts/backends/).

## Two densities

A model carries two densities over the same nodes:

- the **target** $\target(x \mid z)$, which the model *scores*, and
- the **proposal** $\proposal(x \mid z)$, the mechanism it *generates* samples from.

`add_quantity` puts a term in both. `add_proposal` puts a term in $\proposal$ only, and the target's
view of that quantity is attached separately with `add_factor`.

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-model/src/client_ops.rs:124..135}
```

| Call | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `log_density(&points)` | $\log \target$ |
| `log_proposal(&points)` | $\log \proposal$ |
| `log_weight(&points)` | $\log \target - \log \proposal$, compiled and evaluated in one pass |
| `generate(batch)` | a batch of samples drawn through $\proposal$ |

> [!DECISION]
> One graph, two densities. See [Two densities](../../design/two-densities/).

## Derivatives

`log_density_ord1` returns the log-density together with its gradient with respect to the free
quantities, laid out in their tangent coordinates. Second-order information comes as
Hessian–vector products (`log_density_ord2`, `evaluate_hvp`), one direction per call.

